  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nToday and Tonight:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon features a zonal mid/upper \npattern over the eastern two third of the CONUS while the western \nthird continues to hold southwesterly flow that is every so slightly \npropping up heights over the Rocky Mountain Front Range. Looking \ninto the surface pattern locally, we find ourselves in the wake of \nan MCV from morning convection while the prominent surface low \nover west-central Missouri continues to pull northerly winds \nacross our area. With the MCV/convective debris pushing \neastward, we're being left wind increasing clear skies through \nthe afternoon with highs trending towards the lower 80s before \nthings cool down tonight. As the daylight decreases, so too will\nwind speeds, leading to tumbling temperatures as radiational \ncooling ramps up. Convective debris will be incoming from the \nnorthwest, giving some high cloud cover to limit that cooling, \nwhile fog formation is favored near the NE/IA border and points \nsoutheast. Initial model runs indicate that it may not be dense,\nbut patchy coverage of 1 mile or less is expected in that area \nfrom 5 to 10 AM. \n\nMonday and Tuesday:\n\nAfter the aforementioned decaying showers and storms wafting in \nfrom the northwest tomorrow morning, our next chance at showers\nand storms returns Monday afternoon and evening, where a \nshortwave/associated surface boundary shifts southeastward \nacross the Dakotas and pushes a narrow but moist airmass ahead \nof it. Along with increasing CAPE values, the main branch of the\nmid/upper jet stream will be places just to the north of the \narea, helping to increase effective shear levels to over 35 kts.\nScattered storms will generally be steered perpendicular to the\nsurface boundary and orientation of the moving baroclinic zone,\nwith dry lower levels for wind gusts and an elongated hodograph\nfor severe hail. There's just enough low-level curvature to the\nhodograph to facilitate a tornado or two, but slightly elevated\nLCLs may make it hard for more than a couple to reach the \nground. Most of the activity should begin near 5/6 PM in central\nNebraska, moving into northeast Nebraska midnight to 1 AM. Some\nelevated convection is also possible into the overnight hours, \nbut overall intensity will be limited by decreasing instability \nacross northeast Nebraska.\n\nAdditional chances for strong to severe storms develops Tuesday as a \nmore focused cold front shifts southward across the forecast area \nwhile a primary surface low takes shape over western Nebraska. We'll \ncontinue to be in the periphery of potent mid/upper flow that \nincreases slightly and veers throughout the column. With the slow \nprogression of the front, moisture will continue to pool just ahead \nof it and further facilitate strong to severe potential. An \ninitially discrete storm mode will cluster over time as the bulk \nshear vectors become parallel with the boundary. All hazards remain \na concern, with a higher intensity ceiling while being more \nwidespread compared to Monday afternoon. Tuesday afternoon and \nevening continue to look most favorable for the activity, with the \nthreat shifting east-southeast into the overnight hours.\n\nWednesday and Beyond:\n\nWednesday will be the coolest day of the remaining week, with daily \nlow-end chances at showers and storms holding on as the mid/upper \npattern builds a ridge to the west. Temperatures will trend up \nslightly heading into the weekend, but we'll still be cool and \nconformable heading into late August.",
